//? Title: DCGAN: Transposed Maps and Two Batch-Normalization Phases
//? Subtitle: A complete native MNIST experiment with visible moment commits and Adam writes
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A compact convolutional generator maps sixteen Gaussian coordinates to signed28x28
pixels through two learned stride-two transposed convolutions. A strided discriminator
scores real and generated images with a stable logistic game. Each D phase normalizes
one concatenated real/fake batch while stopping G; the following fresh G phase uses
G batch statistics and the updated D's saved statistics. The editable native program
shows all four stores, moment captures, Double.9 moving updates and two Adam states.
It fits1000 balanced MNIST digits for three fixed epochs, visits every fitting row
once per epoch and preserves the original checkpoint, scores, samples and diagnostics.
The short run produces weak blurry structures, and coarse-pixel MMD remains much worse
than a repeated fitting-mean baseline. D's game accuracy does not measure sample quality.
)
\toc

> Follow the learned spatial maps and the two normalization conventions

[Radford, Metz and Chintala (2015/2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.06434)
introduced the DCGAN architecture family. This is a small teaching variant with only
8/4 generator channels and4/8 discriminator channels, rather than a reproduction
of the paper's full architectures or image-quality benchmarks. No true digit labels
enter fitting. Bias-free projection16→392 reshapes to7x7x8; G then learns two4x4,
stride-two transposed maps to14x14x4 and28x28x1. D learns two4x4 stride-two maps
back to14x14x4 and7x7x8, then a raw scalar head. Kernels start from normal(.02);
BN gamma/beta start1/0. G has6873 optimized values, D989. Their saved BN means
and variances add40 values; all21 tensors are checkpointed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dcgan_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
