> Check what a stride-two transposed convolution actually adds

A transposed convolution scatters a learned kernel around each input location and
adds overlaps. It does not repeat pixels and does not invert a downsampling map.
For a2x2 field of ones, an all-one4x4 kernel, SAME padding and stride2, the4x4
output counts are1 at corners,2 along other border positions and4 inside. The
image divides counts by4 only for display; the printed values remain the raw sums.
The actual G learns8→4 and4→1 kernels, then applies BN/ReLU and tanh as described.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dcgan_transpose.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  auto x = n::ones({1, 2, 2, 1}), w = n::ones({4, 4, 1, 1});
  auto out = n::conv2d_transpose(x, w, n::conv_options(2, n::Padding::Same));
  auto expected =
      n::tensor({1., 2., 2., 1., 2., 4., 4., 2., 2., 4., 4., 2., 1., 2., 2., 1.}, {1, 4, 4, 1});
  if (!n::equal_bits(out, expected))
    throw std::runtime_error("transpose hand counts");
  std::print("Raw overlap counts: {}\n", out.values_str());
  n::save_image_grid(out / 4., "build/dcgan_transpose.png", 1, 0, 1, 4, 220 / 255., 32);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Raw overlap counts: [[[[1], [2], [2], [1]], [[2], [4], [4], [2]], [[2], [4], [4], [2]], [[1], [2], [2], [1]]]]
\image(figures/dcgan_transpose.png)
// result_end
\caption(The cropped border has fewer contributions even though the interior overlap count is constant for this kernel/stride pair. This is a supplied hand example, not a learned activation map.)
\alttext(A4x4 grayscale grid shows transposed-convolution overlap counts1,2 and4, normalized only for display.)

> Joint real/fake BN is different from normalizing two pools separately

Take one-channel real values(1,3) and fake values(5,7). Their joint mean is4,
population variance5 and sample variance20/3. Separate means2 and6 erase the
between-pool offset. The source normalizes with population variance plus Double
.001 but captures sample variance for the moving update. Starting from saved
mean0/variance1, retention.9 gives next mean.4 and variance about1.5666667.
`capture_affine_batch_norm` delegates to native fused BN and returns detached
captures without writing those candidates. Its output retains input/gamma/beta
paths. Saved BN stops the moment paths. These are the original fused arithmetic
and [sample-variance capture conventions](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/raw_ops/FusedBatchNormV3).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dcgan_joint_bn.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n         = dm::nn;
  auto              t = n::DType::Float64;
  n::ParameterStore p(0, t), s(0, t);
  auto              x     = n::tensor({1., 3., 5., 7.}, {4, 1}, t);
  auto              joint = n::capture_affine_batch_norm(p, s, "bn", x, true);
  auto first    = n::capture_affine_batch_norm(p, s, "bn", n::slice_axis(x, 0, 0, 2), true);
  auto second   = n::capture_affine_batch_norm(p, s, "bn", n::slice_axis(x, 0, 2, 4), true);
  auto separate = n::concat({first.value, second.value}, 0);
  auto next     = n::batch_norm_moving_values(
      s.at("bn/moving_mean"), s.at("bn/moving_var"), joint.mean, joint.unbiased_variance, .9);
  if (joint.mean.item() != 4 || std::abs(joint.unbiased_variance.item() - 20. / 3) > 1e-12 ||
      s.at("bn/moving_mean").item() != 0)
    throw std::runtime_error("joint BN hand moments");
  std::print(
      "Joint mean={}, sample variance={}; detached candidate mean={}, variance={}. Saved state "
      "remains0/1.\n",
      joint.mean.item(),
      joint.unbiased_variance.item(),
      next.mean.item(),
      next.variance.item());
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.y_limits(-2, 3)
      .lines({1., 2., 3., 4.}, joint.value.to_vector<double>(), "One joint pool", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .points({1., 2., 3., 4.}, joint.value.to_vector<double>(), "", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .lines({1., 2., 3., 4.}, separate.to_vector<double>(), "Separate pools", {220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .points({1., 2., 3., 4.}, separate.to_vector<double>(), "", {220, 38, 38}, 4)
      .x_label("Two real rows, then two fake rows")
      .y_label("Normalized value; affine gamma=1, beta=0")
      .title("Joint BN retains the between-pool offset")
      .save_svg("build/dcgan_joint_bn.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Joint mean=4, sample variance=6.666666666666667; detached candidate mean=0.3999999999999999, variance=1.5666666666666664. Saved state remains0/1.
\image(figures/dcgan_joint_bn.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The joint and separate paths read the same unchanged saved store. The toy supplies its values explicitly and makes no claim about learned discriminator features.)
\alttext(Four supplied values normalized jointly versus in two separate pools, with printed population/sample-moment distinction and detached moving candidates.)

> Use raw logits for a numerically stable game

D minimizes mean softplus(-real_logit)+mean softplus(fake_logit); G minimizes
mean softplus(-fake_logit). The means are separate by pool. There is no sigmoid
followed by a clipped logarithm in these objectives. At a strongly negative fake
logit, the non-saturating G derivative stays close to-1 per example, rather than
vanishing. The full program also checks the original extreme±80 probes and stores
all four logit derivatives. Probabilities are separate diagnostics.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dcgan_logistic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::vector<double> score;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 80; ++i)
    score.push_back(-8. + i * .2);
  auto x = n::from_vector(score, {81});
  auto g = n::softplus(-x), d = n::softplus(x);
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.y_limits(-.2, 10)
      .lines(score, g.to_vector<double>(), "G per-row loss", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .lines(score, d.to_vector<double>(), "D fake per-row loss", {220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .x_label("Raw generated-image logit")
      .y_label("Per-row softplus contribution")
      .title("Stable losses without a clipped probability")
      .save_svg("build/dcgan_logistic.svg");
}
```
